<section class="tale-hero" id="top"><div class="tale-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/stories.html">Stories</a><span>/</span><a href="/deities.html#krishna">Krishna</a></nav><p class="eyebrow">BHAGAVATA PURANA / SKANDHA 10 / CHAPTERS 80–81</p><h1>A humble gift.<br><em>A friendship beyond wealth.</em></h1><p class="tale-deck">Sudama carries four handfuls of flattened rice to Dvaraka, uncertain whether he will even reach Krishna. The welcome that awaits him remembers a shared life no palace can erase.</p><div class="tale-meta"><span>Bhagavata Purana · 10.80–81</span><span>About {{ storyCatalog.sudama.minutes }} min read</span><a href="/sources.html#krishna-sudama">View sources</a></div><a class="button" href="#little">Begin the story</a></div><figure class="tale-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/krishna-sudama-hero.webp" alt="Krishna warmly embracing his travel-worn friend Sudama in an imagined Dvaraka palace while Rukmini and attendants welcome him"><figcaption>KRISHNA WELCOMES SUDAMA<span>AI-GENERATED DEVOTIONAL INTERPRETATION</span></figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana</strong>.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Figures in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>Sudama</dt><dd>Krishna’s learned friend from their years in the household of their teacher Sandipani; inwardly content, materially poor and reluctant to ask for wealth.</dd></div><div><dt>Krishna</dt><dd>Lord of Dvaraka, who rises from Rukmini’s couch to embrace Sudama and honours him before the palace household.</dd></div><div><dt>Sudama’s wife</dt><dd>His devoted companion, weakened by hunger, who urges the visit and gathers a small gift from neighbouring households.</dd></div><div><dt>Rukmini</dt><dd>Krishna’s queen, identified in the narrative with Shri, who personally fans their guest and later stays Krishna’s hand.</dd></div><div><dt>Sandipani</dt><dd>The guru in whose household Krishna and Sudama studied and served together.</dd></div></dl></section>
<div class="tale-layout"><aside class="tale-contents"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS STORY</p><nav aria-label="Story chapters"><a href="#little"><span>01</span>A household with little</a><a href="#gift"><span>02</span>Four handfuls</a><a href="#welcome"><span>03</span>The welcome at Dvaraka</a><a href="#memory"><span>04</span>A night in the forest</a><a href="#offering"><span>05</span>The gift revealed</a><a href="#homeward"><span>06</span>Homeward without asking</a><a href="#abundance"><span>07</span>Abundance without possession</a><a href="#reading-note">Text &amp; interpretation</a><a href="/sources.html#krishna-sudama">Sources &amp; reading notes</a></nav><a class="tale-back" href="/stories.html">Return to the collection</a></aside><article class="tale-body">{% include "reader-tools.njk" %}
<section class="tale-chapter" id="little"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>A household with little</h2></div><p>Among Krishna’s friends is a learned brahmana who lives without attachment to possessions. Whatever arrives through circumstance is enough for him. His clothes are worn, and his wife’s are no better. Hunger has made them both thin.</p><p>His wife approaches him gently. Krishna is your friend, she reminds him, and the refuge of those who turn toward him. Go to Dvaraka. When he sees the need of your household, he will not overlook it.</p><p>Sudama does not set out because wealth has suddenly become his desire. He agrees because seeing Krishna would itself be the greatest gain.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="gift"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>Four handfuls</h2></div><p>Before leaving, Sudama asks whether there is anything in the house fit to offer. There is nothing stored away. His wife goes to neighbouring brahmana households and gathers four handfuls of flattened rice. She ties them into a small piece of cloth and places the bundle in her husband’s hands.</p><p>The gift is materially slight, but it is not careless. It has been sought, gathered and carried with love. Sudama walks toward Dvaraka wondering how he will gain Krishna’s presence at all.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="welcome"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>The welcome at Dvaraka</h2></div><p>Sudama passes through the guarded approaches of the Yadava city and enters one of Krishna’s palaces. Krishna is seated with Rukmini when he sees his friend from a distance.</p><p>He rises at once.</p><p>Krishna crosses the hall, embraces Sudama with both arms and weeps with joy. He seats his friend upon his own couch, washes his feet and receives the water upon his head. Rukmini herself stands beside the weary guest and fans him.</p><p>The palace household is astonished. They see worn cloth and a body marked by poverty; Krishna sees the friend he once served beside. No explanation based on rank can account for the honour before them.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="memory"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>A night in the forest</h2></div><p>Krishna and Sudama take one another’s hands and remember their years in Sandipani’s gurukula. Krishna asks whether Sudama recalls the day their teacher’s wife sent them into the forest to gather fuel.</p><p>A violent storm overtook them. Darkness fell, the ground disappeared beneath water, and wind and rain left them unable to find a path. Through the night they moved together, holding hands in the flooded forest so neither would be lost.</p><p>At dawn Sandipani came searching. Finding his students exhausted and still protecting the firewood, he blessed their devoted service and wished that the sacred learning entrusted to them would remain fruitful in this life and beyond.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/krishna-sudama-gurukula.webp" alt="Young Krishna and Sudama sheltering beneath a forest tree in a monsoon storm while protecting gathered firewood, with an imagined gurukula lamp in the distance" loading="lazy"><figcaption>THE GURUKULA MEMORY · AI-GENERATED NARRATIVE INTERPRETATION</figcaption></figure><p>The memory explains more than any title could. Their friendship was formed through shared discipline, discomfort and service—not through the circumstances in which they now meet.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="offering"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>The gift revealed</h2></div><p>Krishna turns from memory to the present and asks what his friend has brought. A gift offered with devotion, he says, is dear to him however small; an abundance offered without devotion does not please him in the same way.</p><p>Sudama lowers his face. The bundle that seemed precious at home now appears painfully modest within the palace. He cannot bring himself to offer it.</p><p>Krishna knows why he has come. Smiling, he reaches into Sudama’s worn cloth and finds the flattened rice. He calls it dear and takes one handful with delight. When he reaches for another, Rukmini holds his hand. One handful, she says, is enough to bring flourishing in this world and beyond.</p><p>The scene does not measure devotion by the price of its offering. Krishna receives the intention carried inside the cloth; Rukmini’s gesture marks how fully that intention has already been answered.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="homeward"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>Homeward without asking</h2></div><p>Sudama stays the night in the palace, cared for as though he has entered Vaikuntha. The next morning Krishna walks with him for part of the journey and sends him home with affection.</p><p>Sudama has asked for nothing. He carries no visible treasure away. Yet his thoughts are filled not with disappointment, but with Krishna’s embrace, the seat he was given and Rukmini’s service. He wonders at a friendship that could cross such an outward distance without hesitation.</p><p>Perhaps, he thinks, Krishna withheld wealth because riches might produce pride and forgetfulness. Even this thought reveals his freedom from bargaining: he does not turn the visit into a failed exchange.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="abundance"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>Abundance without possession</h2></div><p>Near home, Sudama finds radiant houses, gardens and richly adorned attendants where his simple dwelling had stood. His wife comes forward transformed by abundance. He understands that the change is Krishna’s grace, given without being requested.</p><p>The story does not end with Sudama becoming consumed by possession. He and his wife receive what has come as sacred provision, without clinging to it, and his devotion grows.</p><p>He remembers the scale of Krishna’s friendship: Krishna gives greatly and counts it as little, while receiving a friend’s smallest act as though it were great. Sudama’s deepest wish is not for the house around him, but for friendship, service and loving association with Krishna through every life.</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>What does a true friend see<br><em>before status enters the room?</em></h2><p>Krishna does not wait for Sudama to become presentable, prosperous or persuasive. What relationship in your own life asks to be met with that same freedom from calculation?</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reading-note"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>The gift is welcomed.<br><em>It is not a transaction.</em></h2><p>This page follows the supplied Bhagavata account. Sudama’s wife urges the visit because the household is hungry; Sudama hopes above all to see Krishna. He never voices a request for wealth. Krishna welcomes him, remembers their gurukula service, receives the flattened rice and bestows abundance without being asked.</p><p>The episode should not be reduced to a promise that a small offering guarantees material prosperity. Its conclusion emphasizes Sudama’s detachment, deepening devotion and release from binding action. The closing questions about friendship are editorial reflections.</p><p>The supplied text’s chapter heading names Sudama, while the verses often call him the brahmana or Krishna’s friend. “Childhood friend” is used here in the familiar sense of their earlier student life together, not as a claim that the chapter gives their exact ages.</p><p>The paintings are contemporary devotional interpretations. Dvaraka’s architecture, clothing, faces and the imagined view of Sandipani’s hermitage are artistic choices rather than historical reconstructions.</p><a href="/sources.html#krishna-sudama">Read the supplied-edition references</a></section>
